        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Classification Decisions and Evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- \n",
        "\n",
        "# Classification Evaluation, Statistical Decision Theory, and Threshold-Based Learning -->\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "In statistical learning, classification is fundamentally different from ordinary regression.\n",
        "\n",
        "In regression, the goal is usually to estimate a continuous response accurately.\n",
        "\n",
        "In classification, however, the goal is not merely prediction. Instead, classification involves:\n",
        "\n",
        "- uncertainty\n",
        "- decision making\n",
        "- asymmetric error costs\n",
        "- signal detection\n",
        "- threshold selection\n",
        "\n",
        "This makes classification closely connected to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- statistical decision theory\n",
        "- hypothesis testing\n",
        "- detection theory\n",
        "- the bias-variance tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Metrics such as precision, recall, ROC curves, and AUC are not merely machine learning formulas. They arise naturally from statistical ideas about uncertainty and decision making.\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter develops a unified statistical understanding of classification evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Binary Classification Setup\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the response variable is binary:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y \\in \\{0,1\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $Y=1$ denotes the positive class\n",
        "- $Y=0$ denotes the negative class\n",
        "\n",
        "A classifier produces predictions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat Y \\in \\{0,1\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The outcomes can be summarized using the confusion matrix.\n",
        "\n",
        "| | Predicted Positive | Predicted Negative |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| True Positive Class | TP | FN |\n",
        "| True Negative Class | FP | TN |\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- TP = true positives\n",
        "- FP = false positives\n",
        "- FN = false negatives\n",
        "- TN = true negatives\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Accuracy\n",
        "\n",
        "The most basic classification metric is accuracy:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Accuracy}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{TP+TN}{N}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy measures the proportion of correctly classified observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "Although accuracy is intuitive, it has an important limitation:\n",
        "\n",
        "> It implicitly assumes all mistakes have equal cost.\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice, this assumption is often unrealistic.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- missing cancer may be far worse than a false alarm\n",
        "- falsely flagging a legitimate bank transaction may inconvenience customers\n",
        "- falsely accusing an innocent person may have severe consequences\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus classification evaluation often requires more refined metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Recall}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall measures:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Among all truly positive observations,\n",
        "> how many were successfully detected?\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall is therefore a conditional probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is also called:\n",
        "\n",
        "- sensitivity\n",
        "- true positive rate (TPR)\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Statistical Interpretation of Recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall measures the ability to detect true signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "High recall means:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "few false negatives\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Low recall means:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "many missed signals\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall is closely related to:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Type II error\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "statistical power\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "since:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Power}\n",
        "=\n",
        "1-\\text{Type II Error}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus recall may be interpreted as:\n",
        "\n",
        "> the probability of correctly detecting a true signal.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Precision}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Among all predicted positive observations,\n",
        "> how many are truly positive?\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice that the conditioning direction differs from recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall conditions on the truth.\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision conditions on the prediction.\n",
        "\n",
        "This distinction is extremely important.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Statistical Interpretation of Precision\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "trustworthiness of positive predictions\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "High precision means:\n",
        "\n",
        "- few false positives\n",
        "- reliable positive discoveries\n",
        "\n",
        "Low precision means:\n",
        "\n",
        "- many false alarms\n",
        "- many predicted positives are incorrect\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision is closely related to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- false discovery control\n",
        "- positive predictive value\n",
        "\n",
        "in statistics and medical testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Bayes' Rule Connection\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision and recall are connected through Bayes' theorem.\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\hat Y=1\\mid Y=1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid \\hat Y=1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These are related through:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision depends not only on recall, but also on:\n",
        "\n",
        "- class prevalence\n",
        "- prediction frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "This explains why precision may collapse in highly imbalanced datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Class Imbalance\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose only 1% of observations are positive.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then even a classifier with excellent recall may have poor precision.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- 100 true positives\n",
        "- 900 false positives\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Recall}=1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "but:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Precision}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{100}{1000}\n",
        "=\n",
        "0.1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus most predicted positives are incorrect.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why accuracy alone becomes misleading under severe class imbalance.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Classification Thresholds\n",
        "\n",
        "Many classifiers output probabilities or scores:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(x)=P(Y=1\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "To make a final decision, we choose a threshold:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat Y=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1 & p(x)>c\\\\\n",
        "0 & p(x)\\le c\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $c$ is the classification threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing the threshold changes classification behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision-Recall Tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we lower the threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the classifier predicts positive more aggressively.\n",
        "\n",
        "This usually causes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- recall to increase\n",
        "- precision to decrease\n",
        "\n",
        "because:\n",
        "\n",
        "- more true positives are detected\n",
        "- but more false positives are also produced\n",
        "\n",
        "Conversely, raising the threshold generally causes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- higher precision\n",
        "- lower recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision and recall naturally trade off against each other.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Statistical Decision Theory\n",
        "\n",
        "This tradeoff arises from statistical decision theory.\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification is fundamentally:\n",
        "\n",
        "> decision making under uncertainty.\n",
        "\n",
        "Different applications impose different error costs.\n",
        "\n",
        "The optimal threshold therefore depends on the application.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Medical Screening Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we are screening for cancer.\n",
        "\n",
        "Missing a cancer patient may be extremely costly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus false negatives are dangerous.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore we prioritize:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "high recall\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "even if this produces additional false positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Extra medical testing may be acceptable if it reduces missed diagnoses.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Spam Filtering Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Now consider spam filtering.\n",
        "\n",
        "If precision is low, many legitimate emails will be classified as spam.\n",
        "\n",
        "Users may become frustrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision becomes more important.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Hypothesis Testing Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification may also be interpreted through hypothesis testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H_0:Y=0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "versus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H_1:Y=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "- predicting positive corresponds to rejecting $H_0$\n",
        "- predicting negative corresponds to failing to reject $H_0$\n",
        "\n",
        "Under this interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Classification | Hypothesis Testing |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| False positive | Type I error |\n",
        "| False negative | Type II error |\n",
        "| Recall | Statistical power |\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus recall is essentially the power of a statistical test.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Thresholds and Significance Levels\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification thresholds resemble significance levels in hypothesis testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Lower thresholds correspond to:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "more aggressive rejection\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This increases:\n",
        "\n",
        "- recall\n",
        "- false positives\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly, higher significance levels in hypothesis testing increase rejection frequency.\n",
        "\n",
        "Conversely, conservative thresholds reduce false positives but may miss true signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus classification thresholds and significance levels play similar statistical roles.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# ROC Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "ROC stands for:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Receiver Operating Characteristic\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "An ROC curve plots:\n",
        "\n",
        "- true positive rate (TPR)\n",
        "- versus false positive rate (FPR)\n",
        "\n",
        "across all thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "The true positive rate is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "TPR\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "which is recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "The false positive rate is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "FPR\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{FP}{FP+TN}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# ROC Curves and Hypothesis Testing\n",
        "\n",
        "ROC curves are deeply connected to hypothesis testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall that:\n",
        "\n",
        "- TPR corresponds to power\n",
        "- FPR corresponds to Type I error\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus an ROC curve essentially studies:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "power versus Type I error\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "across all thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is one of the deepest statistical interpretations of ROC analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Interpreting ROC Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "A classifier near the diagonal line behaves almost randomly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Better classifiers push the ROC curve toward the upper-left corner.\n",
        "\n",
        "The ideal classifier would achieve:\n",
        "\n",
        "- TPR = 1\n",
        "- FPR = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "meaning:\n",
        "\n",
        "- perfect detection\n",
        "- no false alarms\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# AUC\n",
        "\n",
        "The area under the ROC curve is called AUC.\n",
        "\n",
        "AUC summarizes ranking performance across all thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "One of the most beautiful statistical interpretations of AUC is:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $s(X^+)$ is the score of a random positive observation\n",
        "- $s(X^-)$ is the score of a random negative observation\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus AUC measures:\n",
        "\n",
        "> the probability that a random positive observation receives a higher score than a random negative observation.\n",
        "\n",
        "This interpretation is fundamentally probabilistic and ranking-based.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why AUC Is Threshold-Free\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy depends on a specific threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "AUC does not.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead, AUC evaluates:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "ranking quality across all thresholds\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This makes AUC particularly useful when the optimal threshold is unknown.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision-Recall Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "A precision-recall curve plots:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision\n",
        "- versus recall\n",
        "\n",
        "across thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "PR curves are especially useful for imbalanced datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# ROC vs PR Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "ROC curves may appear optimistic under severe class imbalance.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- 99% negatives\n",
        "- very small FPR\n",
        "\n",
        "may still generate many false positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "PR curves reflect this problem more directly because they incorporate precision.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus PR curves are often preferred for:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "rare-event detection\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- fraud detection\n",
        "- medical diagnosis\n",
        "- anomaly detection\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# F1 Score\n",
        "\n",
        "The F1 score combines precision and recall:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F1\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{2PR}{P+R}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $P$ = precision\n",
        "- $R$ = recall\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the harmonic mean of precision and recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why Harmonic Mean?\n",
        "\n",
        "The harmonic mean strongly penalizes imbalance.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision = 1\n",
        "- recall = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "then:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F1=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus F1 favors classifiers that balance both quantities.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision, Recall, and the Bias-Variance Tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision and recall are also connected to the bias-variance tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a classifier is extremely conservative.\n",
        "\n",
        "It predicts positive only when highly certain.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision may be high\n",
        "- recall may be low\n",
        "\n",
        "This resembles a high-bias classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classifier avoids risky decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Aggressive Classification and Variance\n",
        "\n",
        "Now suppose the classifier aggressively predicts positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "- recall may increase\n",
        "- precision may decrease\n",
        "\n",
        "because the classifier becomes more sensitive to subtle patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "This resembles lower bias but higher variance.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classifier becomes more flexible but also more unstable.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Thresholds as Complexity Control\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing the classification threshold may itself be viewed as controlling effective complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "A very low threshold produces:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "aggressive positive prediction\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A very high threshold produces:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "conservative positive prediction\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus threshold selection acts like a bias-variance tuning mechanism.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Model Complexity and Precision-Recall Behavior\n",
        "\n",
        "Different model classes naturally produce different precision-recall behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "Highly flexible models:\n",
        "\n",
        "- deep trees\n",
        "- boosting\n",
        "- neural networks\n",
        "\n",
        "may achieve higher recall by detecting subtle patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, they may also generate more false positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simpler models:\n",
        "\n",
        "- linear models\n",
        "- regularized models\n",
        "\n",
        "may achieve higher precision but lower recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision-recall behavior is closely tied to model complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Boosting and Recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting methods such as AdaBoost and XGBoost often aggressively improve recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting repeatedly focuses on difficult observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "As a result, boosting tends to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- reduce false negatives\n",
        "- improve detection power\n",
        "\n",
        "However, overly aggressive boosting may increase false positives if the model begins fitting noise.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus boosting often navigates a delicate precision-recall tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# A Unified Statistical View\n",
        "\n",
        "We may summarize the relationships as follows.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Classification | Statistical Interpretation |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| False positive | Type I error |\n",
        "| False negative | Type II error |\n",
        "| Recall | Statistical power |\n",
        "| Precision | Reliability of discoveries |\n",
        "| Threshold | Significance cutoff |\n",
        "| ROC curve | Power vs Type I error |\n",
        "| AUC | Ranking probability |\n",
        "| Threshold tuning | Bias-variance tradeoff |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final Remarks\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification evaluation is fundamentally statistical.\n",
        "\n",
        "Metrics such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision\n",
        "- recall\n",
        "- ROC curves\n",
        "- AUC\n",
        "- F1 score\n",
        "\n",
        "all arise naturally from:\n",
        "\n",
        "- statistical decision theory\n",
        "- hypothesis testing\n",
        "- uncertainty quantification\n",
        "- asymmetric error costs\n",
        "- model complexity control\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall measures the ability to detect true signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures the reliability of positive discoveries.\n",
        "\n",
        "ROC curves study the tradeoff between detection power and false alarm rate.\n",
        "\n",
        "AUC measures ranking quality across all thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ultimately, classification is not merely about maximizing accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is about making statistically meaningful decisions under uncertainty while balancing competing risks.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calibration and Probability Quality\n",
        "\n",
        "So far, we have discussed:\n",
        "\n",
        "- classification decisions\n",
        "- thresholds\n",
        "- precision and recall\n",
        "- ROC curves\n",
        "- AUC\n",
        "\n",
        "However, these evaluation tools primarily focus on:\n",
        "\n",
        "- classification quality\n",
        "- ranking quality\n",
        "- decision quality\n",
        "\n",
        "They do not directly evaluate whether the predicted probabilities themselves are trustworthy.\n",
        "\n",
        "This leads to an important additional question:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Are the predicted probabilities numerically correct?\n",
        "\n",
        "This question is addressed by calibration.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# What Is Calibration?\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a classifier predicts:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x)=0.8.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A natural interpretation is:\n",
        "\n",
        "> approximately 80% of such observations should truly belong to the positive class.\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration measures whether this interpretation is correct.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus a classifier is well calibrated if:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "predicted probabilities\n",
        "≈\n",
        "observed frequencies\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# A Simple Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a model predicts probability 0.7 for 100 observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the model is well calibrated, then approximately:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "70 out of 100\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "should truly belong to the positive class.\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly:\n",
        "\n",
        "- predicted 0.2 should correspond to roughly 20% positives\n",
        "- predicted 0.9 should correspond to roughly 90% positives\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration therefore concerns:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "probability correctness\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "rather than merely classification accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calibration Versus Classification Accuracy\n",
        "\n",
        "This distinction is extremely important.\n",
        "\n",
        "A classifier may achieve:\n",
        "\n",
        "- high accuracy\n",
        "- high AUC\n",
        "- strong ranking performance\n",
        "\n",
        "while still producing poor probability estimates.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus:\n",
        "\n",
        "> good classification does not necessarily imply good calibration.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# AUC Versus Calibration\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall that AUC measures:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus AUC primarily evaluates:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "ranking quality\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "AUC asks:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Does the classifier rank positive observations above negative observations?\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration asks something very different:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Are the predicted probabilities numerically meaningful?\n",
        "\n",
        "These are distinct properties.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# A Concrete Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider two models.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Model A\n",
        "\n",
        "Predictions for positive observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "0.51\n",
        "0.52\n",
        "0.53\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Predictions for negative observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "0.48\n",
        "0.47\n",
        "0.46\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Model B\n",
        "\n",
        "Predictions for positive observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "0.99\n",
        "0.98\n",
        "0.97\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Predictions for negative observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "0.02\n",
        "0.01\n",
        "0.03\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "Both models may produce nearly identical rankings.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus both may have similar AUC.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, their probability behavior is very different.\n",
        "\n",
        "Model B produces extremely confident predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "If those probabilities are inaccurate, the model may be poorly calibrated despite excellent AUC.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calibration Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "A common visualization tool is the calibration curve, also called the reliability diagram.\n",
        "\n",
        "The basic idea is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Group observations according to predicted probabilities\n",
        "2. Compute empirical positive frequencies within each group\n",
        "3. Compare predicted probabilities with observed frequencies\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Reliability Diagram\n",
        "\n",
        "A reliability diagram plots:\n",
        "\n",
        "- predicted probability\n",
        "- versus observed frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "For a perfectly calibrated classifier:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "predicted probability\n",
        "=\n",
        "observed frequency\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the ideal calibration curve is the 45-degree line.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Interpretation of Calibration Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "If the curve lies below the diagonal:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "model is overconfident\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- predicted probability = 0.9\n",
        "- actual frequency = 0.7\n",
        "\n",
        "The model predicts probabilities that are too extreme.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "If the curve lies above the diagonal:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "model is underconfident\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- predicted probability = 0.3\n",
        "- actual frequency = 0.5\n",
        "\n",
        "The model predicts probabilities that are too conservative.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Logistic Regression and Calibration\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression often produces reasonably calibrated probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "This occurs because logistic regression directly models:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "using maximum likelihood estimation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Under reasonable model assumptions, the estimated probabilities often correspond fairly well to empirical frequencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus logistic regression is frequently used as a baseline calibrated classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Boosting and Calibration\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting methods such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- AdaBoost\n",
        "- Gradient Boosting\n",
        "- XGBoost\n",
        "\n",
        "often achieve excellent classification accuracy and AUC.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, boosting models are frequently less well calibrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why Boosting May Be Poorly Calibrated\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting repeatedly pushes observations farther from the decision boundary.\n",
        "\n",
        "In AdaBoost, this is closely related to margin growth.\n",
        "\n",
        "As boosting continues, predicted scores may become increasingly extreme.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "0.001\n",
        "0.999\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "These extreme predictions improve separation and ranking quality, but they do not necessarily correspond to true probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus boosting often produces:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "excellent ranking\n",
        "but overconfident probabilities\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Random Forests and Calibration\n",
        "\n",
        "Random forests often produce probabilities that are somewhat conservative.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because predictions are averaged across many trees, the resulting probabilities may shrink toward the center.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus random forests sometimes exhibit:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "underconfident probabilities\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "even when classification performance is strong.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calibration and the Bias-Variance Tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration is also connected to the bias-variance tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "Very flexible models may overfit noise and produce:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "extreme probabilities\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This increases variance and may damage calibration.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simpler models may produce:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "smoother probability estimates\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "which are sometimes better calibrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus probability calibration depends not only on classification accuracy, but also on model complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Proper Scoring Rules\n",
        "\n",
        "Metrics such as accuracy and AUC evaluate decisions or rankings.\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration requires evaluation of probability quality itself.\n",
        "\n",
        "This motivates proper scoring rules.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Brier Score\n",
        "\n",
        "One important scoring rule is the Brier score:\n",
        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $y_i$ is the true label\n",
        "- $p_i$ is the predicted probability\n",
        "\n",
        "The Brier score measures the quality of probabilistic forecasts.\n",
        "\n",
        "Lower Brier scores indicate better probability estimates.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calibration Methods\n",
        "\n",
        "If a classifier is poorly calibrated, post-processing methods may improve calibration.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two common approaches are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Platt scaling\n",
        "- isotonic regression\n",
        "\n",
        "These methods adjust predicted scores to better match empirical frequencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Platt Scaling\n",
        "\n",
        "Platt scaling fits a logistic regression model to the classifier scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the original classifier outputs score:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Platt scaling models:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid s(x))\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "using logistic regression.\n",
        "\n",
        "This often improves probability calibration while preserving ranking quality.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Isotonic Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Isotonic regression is a nonparametric calibration method.\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike logistic calibration, isotonic regression does not assume a sigmoid shape.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead, it learns a monotone transformation of the predicted scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "This method is more flexible but may overfit on small datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Three Different Evaluation Goals\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification evaluation actually involves several distinct goals.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Decision Quality\n",
        "\n",
        "Measured by:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision\n",
        "- recall\n",
        "- F1 score\n",
        "\n",
        "These metrics evaluate threshold-based decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Ranking Quality\n",
        "\n",
        "Measured by:\n",
        "\n",
        "- ROC curves\n",
        "- AUC\n",
        "\n",
        "These metrics evaluate ordering performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Probability Quality\n",
        "\n",
        "Measured by:\n",
        "\n",
        "- calibration curves\n",
        "- Brier score\n",
        "\n",
        "These metrics evaluate probability correctness.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# A Unified Perspective\n",
        "\n",
        "These perspectives answer different statistical questions.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Evaluation Goal | Main Question |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Accuracy | How often are predictions correct? |\n",
        "| Precision/Recall | How good are the classification decisions? |\n",
        "| ROC/AUC | How well are observations ranked? |\n",
        "| Calibration | Are probabilities trustworthy? |\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus classification evaluation is inherently multidimensional.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final Remarks\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration is one of the most important and most overlooked aspects of classification.\n",
        "\n",
        "A classifier may achieve:\n",
        "\n",
        "- excellent accuracy\n",
        "- excellent AUC\n",
        "- excellent ranking performance\n",
        "\n",
        "while still producing unreliable probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "This distinction is especially important in applications where probabilities themselves drive decisions, such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- medicine\n",
        "- finance\n",
        "- risk assessment\n",
        "- weather forecasting\n",
        "\n",
        "Calibration therefore completes the statistical picture of classification evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification models should be evaluated not only by:\n",
        "\n",
        "- how well they classify\n",
        "- how well they rank\n",
        "\n",
        "but also by:\n",
        "\n",
        "> how well their predicted probabilities reflect reality."
